# Test audio generator — local development

LOG_LEVEL=info
PORT=8005
HOST=127.0.0.1

# --- Inbound service key ---------------------------------------------------
# Presented by admin-server's test-audio BFF as `Authorization: Bearer`, and it
# is the ONLY thing between a caller on the backend network and the ability to
# point a synthetic audio source at a live room. Must match admin-server's
# TEST_AUDIO_SERVICE_KEY.
#
# The service REFUSES TO START on an empty or CHANGEME value. An empty
# configured key matches the empty credential an unauthenticated caller
# presents as `Authorization: Bearer `, which is an auth bypass rather than a
# closed door, and compose substitutes a blank string for an unset variable —
# so failing closed is the only safe reading of "not set".
#
# This service is not proxied by nginx and sits on the backend network only.
TEST_AUDIO_SERVICE_KEY=CHANGEME

# --- The two devices' tokens -----------------------------------------------
# Each device authenticates as a registered ScribeAR device, joins whatever
# session is active in ITS OWN device's room, and streams as a source. Format
# is the DEVICE_TOKEN cookie value, {deviceUid}:{secret}. Empty — the default —
# means that device reports `configured: false` and refuses to start; the admin
# page shows it as a disabled card.
#
# SAFETY: these devices stream synthetic audio into a REAL session. Each device
# MUST belong to its own DEDICATED TEST ROOM. If you point one at a teaching
# room, fixture speech will be transcribed into that lecture's live captions,
# silently, with nothing in the stack to notice it.
#
# A device token reaches ONLY ITS OWN DEVICE'S ROOM. Neither device has any way
# to name another room, so the device-to-room assignment made once at
# provisioning time is the ENTIRE SAFETY BOUNDARY. Getting it wrong is a
# provisioning mistake, not a runtime one, and it cannot be undone by anything
# at runtime — which is why `deployment/provision-test-audio.sh` refuses to
# touch an existing non-test room.
#
# TWO ROOMS, NOT ONE. A room has exactly one source device, and both devices
# must be able to run at once, so they cannot share.
#
# One-time setup — use `deployment/provision-test-audio.sh`, which does all of
# this and prints the two lines to paste. By hand it is:
#   1. POST /api/session-manager/v1/device-management/register-device
#      with `Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_API_KEY` -> {activationCode}
#   2. POST /api/session-manager/v1/device-management/activate-device
#      with {activationCode}; read the DEVICE_TOKEN cookie from Set-Cookie
#   3. Create TEST-AUDIO-GOOD and TEST-AUDIO-FAULT, each with its own device as
#      its source device
#   4. Give each room a standing schedule so a session is active to stream into
#   5. Put the two cookie values below
#
# This service holds nothing else: no ADMIN_API_KEY (which would let it create
# sessions in any room) and no SESSION_TOKEN_SIGNING_KEY (which would let it
# forge a token for any session in the fleet). A synthetic audio source that
# could mint arbitrary credentials would be a far larger liability than
# anything it exists to demonstrate.
TEST_AUDIO_GOOD_DEVICE_TOKEN=
TEST_AUDIO_FAULT_DEVICE_TOKEN=

# --- Upstreams -------------------------------------------------------------
SESSION_MANAGER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8001
NODE_SERVER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8002

# --- Run bounds ------------------------------------------------------------
# Hard ceiling on one run, in seconds. Every run auto-stops at its own
# durationSec with no further instruction, and this bounds what may be asked
# for — a request above it is a 422 naming the cap, not a silent clamp.
#
# This is the AUTHORITATIVE cap. admin-server's schema rejects only absurd
# values (86400) and says so, precisely so that a deployment lowering this
# number is obeyed rather than contradicted. Nothing else stops a forgotten
# device streaming into a room overnight.
TEST_AUDIO_MAX_DURATION_SEC=1800
TEST_AUDIO_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SEC=5
TEST_AUDIO_UPSTREAM_WAIT_SEC=20
# How often each device re-reads which room it belongs to. On a timer rather
# than per request: GET /devices is polled every 3 s by an open admin page, and
# the room changes only when someone re-provisions a device.
TEST_AUDIO_ROOM_REFRESH_SEC=60

# --- Audio -----------------------------------------------------------------
# 100 ms matches the kiosk's AUDIO_CHUNK_MS, so these devices frame audio at
# the same rate real source devices do — which is what makes a fault
# reproduced here a fault a real source could produce.
TEST_AUDIO_CHUNK_MS=100

# MUST equal sample_rate / num_channels in the test rooms' sessions'
# transcriptionStreamConfig. The transcription service raises on a mismatch
# instead of resampling, so a wrong value here means every frame is rejected.
# Each clip is checked against these at load rather than one frame at a time.
TEST_AUDIO_SAMPLE_RATE=16000
TEST_AUDIO_CHANNELS=1

# Paths as laid down by the Dockerfile. For local dev, point at the repo copy:
#   ../../test_audio_files/speech/harvard_16k_mono.wav
TEST_AUDIO_HARVARD_PATH=/app/test_audio_files/speech/harvard_16k_mono.wav
TEST_AUDIO_APOLLO_PATH=/app/test_audio_files/speech/apollo11_dialogue_16k_mono.wav

# --- The longform clip -----------------------------------------------------
# The third clip the `good` device offers, ~5 minutes. Built at image-build
# time and NOT committed: 5 minutes of 16 kHz mono WAV is ~9.6 MB of derived
# audio. If the file is missing at runtime it is built on first use instead,
# which is what a local `npm run dev` always does.
#
# It exists because the two committed fixtures are 33.6 s and 50 s. Whisper's
# decoder is conditioned on its own recent output, so a loop that short walks
# the same sentences past the model every half minute and captions start to
# rhyme with themselves — you cannot then tell the model's failure mode from
# the fixture's.
#
# The default source is "Some Mistakes About Economics" (1896) read by Brian
# Salmons, from the Ralat Readings collection on archive.org: Public Domain
# Mark 1.0, single speaker, already exactly 16 kHz mono 16-bit PCM, 7m11s.
# LibriVox itself could not be used despite being the obvious candidate — it
# publishes MP3 and Ogg only, and there is no audio decoder here.
#
# SET THIS EMPTY on a build host with no egress. The build then concatenates
# the two committed fixtures in Thue-Morse order — chosen because it is
# overlap-free, so no block of segments repeats three times in a row at any
# scale — and says so in its output. Nothing fails either way.
#
# A source that is not already 16 kHz mono 16-bit PCM is REJECTED rather than
# converted. No resampler is shipped on purpose: decimating without an
# anti-alias filter folds everything above 8 kHz into the speech band, and a
# clip whose entire job is to be clean reference speech is the last place to
# put aliasing.
TEST_AUDIO_LONGFORM_PATH=/app/test_audio_files/longform/longform_16k_mono.wav
TEST_AUDIO_LONGFORM_URL=https://archive.org/download/RalatEconomicsMistakes/RalatEconomicsMistakes.wav
TEST_AUDIO_LONGFORM_SEC=300
TEST_AUDIO_LONGFORM_TIMEOUT_SEC=60

# --- The fault device ------------------------------------------------------
# Which clip the `fault` device streams. FaultParams has no clip knob — PLAN
# §2.2 is one knob per fault and nothing else — so the source material is
# configuration. `harvard` by default: it is the fixture with a reference
# transcript, so a fault's effect on the words can be judged against something.
TEST_AUDIO_FAULT_CLIP=harvard

# Seed for the fault engine's draws. Fixed so a run an operator reports can be
# reproduced exactly: "30% drop, seed 1" is a specific sequence of dropped
# frames rather than a distribution. Change it to see a different one.
TEST_AUDIO_RNG_SEED=1
